## Toolchain (you are OFFLINE — the harness handles all network)

- Python: `.venv/bin/python` — pandas, scipy, statsmodels, requests preinstalled (pinned
  in requirements.txt). Never pip install; there is no network in your sandbox.
- The model runner: `llama-completion -m data/models/qwen2.5-0.5b-instruct-q8_0.gguf
  -f <promptfile> -n <max_tokens> --temp 0 --seed 42 -c 9216 --no-display-prompt
  --simple-io` (llama.cpp; NOTE: llama-cli is interactive-only in this build — never
  use it in scripts). Call it via subprocess from analysis scripts.
- TWO-TIER PIPELINE (inference is expensive; aggregation is cheap):
  `make probes` runs `analysis/run_probes.py` (you create it) — generates all prompt
  files from seeded code, runs the model on each, writes raw outputs to `data/probes/`.
  Run it whenever you change the experiment grid. `make results` runs
  `analysis/run_all.py` — reads `data/probes/`, scores and aggregates into results.json
  (contract: analysis/RESULTS_SCHEMA.md). It must be FAST (< 1 min) and deterministic:
  the pipeline-fresh gate re-runs it every lap. `make values` regenerates
  paper/values.tex. `make paper` compiles the ICML PDF via tectonic.
  `make clean-deep && make all` is the full clean-room proof (re-runs all inference,
  ≤ 45 min) — the harness runs it before final submission; hand-edited probe outputs
  will be exposed there and in the public git history.
- Citations: you propose entries in paper/refs.bib; the HARNESS verifies them against
  Crossref/Semantic Scholar after you exit. Already-verified entries are cached in
  data/cache/citations/. Do not invent papers — unresolvable entries fail the gate.
- The frozen model: data/models/ (CHECKSUMS.txt). The literature library:
  data/exemplars/ — metadata JSON + full texts; the context-degradation wing is your
  Related Work seed. READ the full texts before writing Related Work.

## Hard rules (gates will enforce these — violations waste an iteration)

1. Every number, percentage, table cell, and figure in the paper comes from `results.json`,
   written only by scripts in `analysis/`. Prose uses macros from `paper/values.tex`
   (generated by `analysis/make_values.py`). Never type a numeric literal in `paper/*.tex`
   except a publication year. Use protected `paper/model.tex` macros for the model identity;
   put other fixed configuration numbers in results.json too.
2. Every entry in `paper/refs.bib` must be a real paper that resolves via Crossref or
